"""Check the wasm32 clippy guard: its configuration, the lint settings that
could switch it off, and that it refuses a call to each entry.

The guard is the clippy step of the WebAssembly workflow, run with
CLIPPY_CONF_DIR pointing at devtools/wasm-clippy, whose clippy.toml lists
methods and types. A path there that does not resolve only makes clippy print
a warning, and under -D warnings the step still exits 0 (clippy 0.1.97), so a
misspelt entry would leave the step green with that call unguarded. The step
also runs clippy's await_holding_lock and await_holding_refcell_ref lints,
which warn by default and report a std Mutex or RwLock guard, or a RefCell
borrow, held across an .await; on wasm32-unknown-unknown such code traps when
another task takes a conflicting lock or borrow of the same value (std's Mutex
panics with "cannot recursively acquire mutex"). Allowing clippy::all,
clippy::style, clippy::suspicious or warnings, one of the two disallowed lints
or one of those two lints switches the guard off for the code the setting
covers, and the step again exits 0. This script fails, with one line per
problem, unless all of these hold:

1. clippy.toml holds only `disallowed-methods` and `disallowed-types`; each
   entry is a table with a `path` and a `reason` and nothing else; no path is
   listed twice; each entry has a probe below and each probe names an entry.
2. In the crates the wasm build compiles (extenddb-wasm-adapter and the
   workspace crates it depends on for wasm32, read from `cargo metadata`), no
   source file under src/ allows or expects clippy::all, clippy::style,
   clippy::suspicious or warnings, nor clippy::await_holding_lock or
   clippy::await_holding_refcell_ref with or without a reason, nor
   clippy::disallowed_methods or clippy::disallowed_types (or their former
   names) without a `reason` that holds more than whitespace. The one
   exception is a catalog migration's code-body module: CONTRIBUTING.md
   ("Adding a catalog migration") declares each one, in src/migrations/mod.rs,
   with #[rustfmt::skip] and #[allow(clippy::all, deprecated)], because its
   body is checksummed once released and no lint may ask for an edit. An allow
   of clippy::all is accepted only in that exact form, on a `mod mNNNN_<name>;`
   line of a src/migrations/mod.rs whose src/migrations/mNNNN_<name>.rs
   exists; the guard does not see those bodies, and the headless parity
   corpus runs every migration when it creates an engine. The `[lints]` tables of those crates and the
   workspace's `[workspace.lints]` allow none of these lints, and
   .cargo/config.toml passes rustc no flag that allows lints or caps them.
   SETTING_PROBES lists lint settings in each of these spellings (attribute,
   inner attribute, cfg_attr, `[lints]` table, rustflags); the functions that
   read the crates' files must refuse or accept each one as the list says.
   Those functions read lint settings as they are written in the files and
   expand no macro: a setting that a macro builds from its input (for example
   `#[$level(clippy::all)]` in a macro_rules! body), or that a procedural
   macro emits, is not seen.
3. In a copy of the working tree (the files git tracks or does not ignore,
   uncommitted edits included), with every probe added to a source file of
   PROBE_CRATE, the clippy step of the workflow fails with clippy's
   disallowed-method or disallowed-type error naming the probed path inside
   each probe, and prints no warning about clippy.toml.

The copy is built with the caller's clippy.toml and the caller's cargo target
directory, so cargo reuses what the clippy step already checked. Nothing in
the caller's working tree is written. The copy is removed on exit, also on
SIGINT, SIGTERM and SIGHUP, and the clippy run is stopped with it. Some
probes reach the method through an import or a re-export
(`use tokio::time; time::sleep`, `tokio::spawn`), as code usually does.

Needs Python 3.11 or later (for tomllib) and git. Run from anywhere in the
repository; exit status 0 means every check passed.
"""

